---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { AUTHOR, CITE_AS, COUNTS, GENERATED_LONG, METHOD, MODES, SOURCES, modeTitle } from '../components/home/bench';
import { fmUrl, breadcrumbs } from '../lib/seo';

const description =
  "SAICA-Bench, by Vasyl Rakivnenko (Stanford University), measures what supervision tools and LLMs flag in the work of AI coding agents. Open data, CC-BY-4.0.";
const jsonld = [
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', ...AUTHOR, sameAs: ['https://www.linkedin.com/in/rakivnenkov', 'https://github.com/vasylrakivnenko'] },
  breadcrumbs([['SAICA-Bench', '/'], ['About', '/about/']]),
];
---

<BaseLayout title="About SAICA-Bench: Author, Data Sources and License" rawTitle description={description} jsonld={jsonld}>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl">
    SAICA-Bench measures what reviewers flag when they are shown the work of AI coding agents, on {COUNTS.modes}
    failure modes: fabricated code, test manipulation, obsolescence, scope creep and more. It runs free programs
    (type checkers, linters), packaged AI tools and plain language models on labelled samples and reports what each
    flagged, with a 95% interval and the cost. It is built by Vasyl Rakivnenko (Stanford University).
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl">
    {METHOD.setting} {COUNTS.constructed} of the {COUNTS.modes} sets have bad samples that were planted on purpose
    and {COUNTS.agent_output} are real agent output; the <a href="/benchmark/">benchmark</a> page says how each set was
    made and lists the threats to validity, and the <a href="/findings/">findings report</a> says what the results
    do and do not show.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl">
    The failure-mode taxonomy and the tool catalog come from our knowledge graph of supervision tools
    (<a href="/graph/">graph</a>). A failure-mode tag on a tool is the catalog's claim about what the tool is built
    for; a benchmark row is a measurement of one configuration on one set. A tool that was benchmarked but is not in
    the catalog is marked as such. Code and results are on
    <a href="https://github.com/vasylrakivnenko/SAICA" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>; these pages show {CITE_AS}, built
    {GENERATED_LONG}.
  </p>

  <h2 id="data">Data sources</h2>
  <p class="max-w-3xl">
    The samples come from {SOURCES.map((s, i, all) => (
      <>{i > 0 && (i === all.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ')}<a href={s.url} rel="noopener">{s.name}</a>{s.public_since && ` (public since ${s.public_since})`}</>
    ))}. Per failure mode:
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-2 max-w-3xl list-disc pl-5 text-sm leading-6">
    {MODES.map((m) => <li><a href={fmUrl(m.id)}>{modeTitle(m)}</a>: {m.dataset}. Bad samples: {m.provenance.author}.</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl">
    Code is Apache-2.0 and the results data CC-BY-4.0; the source datasets keep their own licenses.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <p class="max-w-3xl">
    Questions, corrections or a tool to add: <a href="https://github.com/vasylrakivnenko/SAICA/issues" rel="noopener">open
    an issue</a> or email Vasyl Rakivnenko at <a href="mailto:vasyl@stanford.edu">vasyl@stanford.edu</a>. You can also find him on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/rakivnenkov" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
